cartridge "SCAVENGER HUNT/BOB BLACKMER/1987"

' SCAVENGER HUNT
' A ColecoVision game based on "Scavenger Hunt" by Bob Blackmer, Ahoy!
' May 1987 (Commodore 64, machine code). Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an
' AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a byte-for-byte port: the rules are the original's (read from its
' machine code, orig/scavengerhunt.ml.dis), the game is rebuilt for the
' ColecoVision: a hunter in two-colour sprites that walks a pixel at a time,
' ladders, brick and item tiles, sound effects.
'
' The game: two hunters race through the same building, each seen in his
' own window. The building has nine corridors joined by ladders. Each
' hunter has a list of six things to find (rings, balls, ladders and boxes
' in white, red or green, and the barber pole). Walk over a thing on your
' list to take it; whoever finds all six first wins. A thing taken by one
' hunter is gone for the other.
'
' From the original (orig/): the split screen, a window over the building
' for each player with his list beside it; the building, 55 columns wide,
' nine corridors three rows high and two brick rows between them; one shaft
' between corridors 1-2, 3-4, 5-6 and 7-8 (in the middle), two between 2-3,
' 4-5, 6-7 and 8-9 (left and right); 63 places, seven in each corridor,
' eight columns apart, filled with the thirteen kinds in turn (about five
' of each) and shuffled; six things a list, drawn at random; walking over a
' thing on your list takes it (others are left alone); the things are
' shared; both hunters start in the middle corridor; the first to finish
' his list wins; joystick in each port; no time limit, no hazards.
'
' Liberties: the screen is redrawn for the 32 x 24 screen (14-column
' windows instead of 17); the C64 moved a character a step (about 25 steps
' a second); here the hunter walks 1.5 pixels a frame (11 columns a second)
' and the window follows a column at a time; pushing up or down near a
' ladder slides the hunter onto it (the C64 needed the exact column); a
' list never holds the same kind twice (on the C64 it could, and two lists
' could then ask for more of a kind than the building holds, so that
' nobody could finish); the shafts are ladders; the tune is played on the
' title only (the C64 played it all through the game); one player can race
' the computer (it knows where things are, walks slower and stops after
' every find); a side button pauses; the 2 x 2 picture the C64 put in a
' random corridor (a decoration) is left out.
'
' Controls: joystick only. Left and right walk along a corridor, up and down
' climb a ladder, a side button pauses. Player 1 uses controller 1, player
' 2 controller 2 (one player: either). On the title, up and down choose,
' left, right or a button change the players, a button starts.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` after every `clear
' sprites`. Own xorshift random numbers.
'
' Workarounds for Amy bugs actnaut-const-product removed 2026-11-01.

